---
import "../styles/components/intake-form.css";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import {
  cases,
  type LearningVerdict,
} from "../content/casebook";

// The contribution section states how the casebook's institutions responded,
// so the tally comes from the cases and moves when a case is added.
const learning: Record<LearningVerdict, number> = {
  learned: 0,
  partial: 0,
  absorbed: 0,
};
for (const entry of cases) {
  learning[entry.learningOutcome.verdict] += 1;
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Ethotechnics — Institute and Studio"
  description="What the Ethotechnics framework is, where it comes from, and how the open Institute and Studio practice fit together."
  publishedTime="2026-05-26T00:00:00.000Z"
>
  <section class="about-hero stack-lg">
    <div class="about-hero__layout">
      <div class="about-hero__main stack-md">
        <p class="eyebrow">About the framework</p>
        <h1>
          An engineering discipline for keeping automated decisions answerable
        </h1>
        <p class="section-lead">
          Institutions absorb the evidence that their automated systems are
          wrong. A worker quietly fixes the error. An appeal settles one case.
          The rule never changes. Ethotechnics names that mechanism, scores it
          in public cases, and specifies the records that force the evidence
          back to the rule.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          The formal definition: keeping capability, authority, evidence,
          dependency, standing, and correction coupled tightly enough that increasing
          machine agency does not silently become unreviewable institutional
          power. Those six are the safeguards the casebook scores. The framework
          is written by <strong>Kanav Jain</strong> and
          published openly as standards, a glossary, and diagnostics.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-hero__flourish" aria-hidden="true">
        <Logo class="about-hero__logo" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Institute and Studio</p>
    <h2>How the Institute and Studio fit together</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Institute</strong> is the public, open-access commons:
      standards, glossary, diagnostics, and field notes are free to use, cite,
      and extend.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Studio</strong> provides clinical AI safety evaluations,
      VC diligence, and governance advisory through commissioned engagements. The
      Institute publishes the open framework; the Studio applies it to specific systems
      and organizations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Visit Ethotechnics Studio →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What existing standards don't cover</p>
    <h2>The gap Ethotechnics addresses</h2>
    <p>
      The NIST AI RMF tells you to manage risk. The EU AI Act tells you to
      document compliance and to keep a way to halt a high-risk system.
      ISO/IEC 42001 tells you to build a management system. The OECD AI
      Principles tell you to be responsible. The GDPR gives a person the right
      to contest one decision. None of them asks whether a challenge that
      succeeds changes the rule, whether permission to act expires, or who
      carries the cost of errors while the system runs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing frameworks govern through what an institution writes down:
      documents, roles, reviews, and attestations. Ethotechnics governs through
      records a running system has to keep: grants that expire unless renewed,
      objections that are owed a revision or a reasoned refusal, a published
      count of the time the system costs people, and a named person who can
      halt it.
    </p>
    <div class="mapping-table__wrapper">
      <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--compare">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Existing standard says</th>
            <th scope="col">Ethotechnics requires</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Existing standard says"
              >"Contest the decision" <span class="small muted">(GDPR, Art. 22(3))</span></td
            >
            <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">A revision or a reasoned refusal for every objection, on the record, within a set clock</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Existing standard says"
              >"Review the management system at planned intervals" <span class="small muted">(ISO/IEC 42001, cl. 9.3)</span></td
            >
            <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">A grant that expires unless someone renews it on evidence stated in advance, and a new authorization for every widening of scope</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Existing standard says"
              >"Characterize impacts on individuals and communities" <span class="small muted">(NIST AI RMF, MAP 5)</span></td
            >
            <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">A published measure of the time the system costs people, and standing for whoever absorbs its errors</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Existing standard says"
              >"Maintain human oversight" <span class="small muted">(EU AI Act, Art. 14)</span></td
            >
            <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">A named person with authority to halt it, a tested halt path, and a recovery deadline</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Existing standard says"
              >"Implement responsible AI principles" <span class="small muted">(OECD AI Principles)</span></td
            >
            <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires">Binding escalation: a named owner, a deadline, and a set action, or the system falls back to a safe mode</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      Ethotechnics does not compete with these standards on whether governance
      is "important." It addresses what they leave open:
      <a href="/standards/#comparison">see the full comparison →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The differentiation is not only against standards but against four nearby
      fields. AI ethics asks whether systems are fair, transparent, private, or
      aligned. Safety engineering asks whether they malfunction. Human-centered
      design asks whether they work for their users. Governance asks who
      oversees them. Ethotechnics cuts across all four and asks something prior:
      how should technical systems be built so that increases in capability do
      not automatically become increases in domination? A system can be
      accurate, transparent, human-supervised, democratically authorized, and
      socially beneficial, and still be bad — if its success depends on quietly
      shifting work and cost onto the people it serves, on appeals nobody can
      use, or on authority expanded beyond what was justified. What the discipline considers low-leverage is catalogued at{
        " "
      }
      <a href="/low-leverage">low-leverage work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The governance landscape</p>
    <h2>Where Ethotechnics fits</h2>
    <p>
      Enterprises buy compliance platforms, evaluation tools, and guardrail
      systems to meet new regulatory requirements. Each of those products needs
      something to be measured against.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics is not a product. It publishes benchmarks and test cases that
      governance platforms can be evaluated against — the role NIST frameworks
      play for compliance tools, or OWASP standards for security tools. Those
      bodies hold that role because institutions adopted them. Ethotechnics is
      proposed for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Institute provides the open standards. The Studio provides
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        evaluations and diligence →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it is for</p>
    <h2>Relying on a system without being ruled by it</h2>
    <p>
      Institutions already rely on things they cannot fully inspect: colleagues,
      suppliers, instruments, the last shift's notes. That reliance is what
      makes an institution more than one person, and nothing here argues against
      it. What the framework is for is keeping reliance on a system from turning
      into subordination to it, in either direction. The institution should be
      able to depend on a system without losing the capacity to question,
      narrow, or replace it. The people the system's decisions land on should be
      able to depend on the institution without losing the standing to object
      and be answered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every law on the site is a constraint that keeps one of those two
      dependences from converting into command. Capability does not become
      authority; a quiet year does not become renewal; a captive population does
      not become a population that may be directed. The
      <a href="/standards/laws">laws</a> state the constraints, the
      <a href="/standards/core-axioms">axioms</a> state what they are owed to, and
      the <a href="/research/theory">theory essays</a> say why. A reader can adopt
      the constraints without the ideal. The ideal is what they add up to.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The compact ambition: systems that can keep becoming more capable without
      becoming more entitled to rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The underlying project</p>
    <h2>Power that stays correctable, because every institution gets things wrong</h2>
    <p>
      Strip the technology away and the underlying claim concerns the
      relationship among knowledge, power, dependence, burden, and correction.
      The question it answers: what kinds of power does a technical system
      create, who bears its failures, and what prevents that power from becoming
      final? The chain behind it is short. Institutions must represent reality
      to act. Representation is necessarily incomplete. Incompleteness creates
      residual burdens. Those burdens are distributed politically. Power becomes
      dominating when it externalizes them while insulating itself from
      correction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Domination, in this frame, is power insulated from{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/consequential-contradiction"
        >consequential contradiction</a
      >: the institution defines the relevant reality, acts on its definition,
      pushes the residual downward, controls what counts as evidence against
      itself, and cannot be forced to revise by those it gets wrong. The
      epistemology is fallibilist rather than skeptical: knowledge stays
      defeasible because <a href="/glossary/excluded-case">the excluded case</a> is
      potentially evidence about the category, and that fallibilism is material —
      a system is not meaningfully open to correction if the people who know it is
      wrong must destroy themselves to prove it.
    </p>
    <p>
      No arrangement is innocent by design: markets, states,
      democratic majorities, expertise, participation, and emancipatory goals
      all face the same permanent question — whatever arrangement you favor,
      what happens when it gets someone wrong? The five concerns of the design
      discipline follow from that, and each is a working part of this site:
      jurisdiction (<a href="/glossary/non-conversion-principle"
        >what the institution may decide at all</a
      >), representation (<a href="/glossary/safe-incompleteness"
        >what it models and how that can be contested</a
      >), residual custody (<a href="/glossary/error-bearing-party"
        >who carries what it cannot represent</a
      >), corrigibility (<a href="/glossary/exception-learning"
        >whether error travels back into the rule</a
      >), and non-finality (<a href="/glossary/non-finality"
        >whether its decisions stay reopenable</a
      >).
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The compact form of the whole project: how can institutions be powerful
      enough to act without becoming powerful enough to define away the evidence
      that they are wrong?
    </p>
  </section>

