  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Intellectual lineage and distinctive contribution</p>
    <h2>Where the argument comes from, and where it departs</h2>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics does not claim originality for the proposition that human
      struggle should be reduced, or that institutions exploit unpriced labor to
      absorb their own failures. Those insights belong to established
      traditions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Post-work socialism and Marx:</strong> The distinction between the
        realm of necessity and the realm of freedom, establishing that technical productivity
        should reduce compulsory labor rather than intensify it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The social model of disability:</strong> Shifting attention away from
        demanding extraordinary individual coping toward altering disabling environments
        and institutional barriers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Relational egalitarianism (Elizabeth Anderson):</strong> The principle
        that individuals are entitled to the social conditions of equal standing and
        ordinary dignity without having to prove exceptional merit or endurance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Feminist care ethics and social reproduction theory (Joan Tronto,
          Nancy Fraser, Eva Kittay, Alison Reiheld):</strong
        > Analyzing how formal economic success depends on unpriced care labor and
        identifying "privileged irresponsibility" — the systemic offloading of maintenance
        work onto others.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Administrative burden theory (Pamela Herd, Donald Moynihan):</strong
        >
        Empirical measurement of the learning, compliance, and psychological costs
        imposed on citizens by state and corporate processes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Republican political theory (Philip Pettit):</strong> Freedom as non-domination,
        demonstrating that making lives easier does not make people free if they remain
        subject to unchecked, arbitrary institutional discretion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Resilience engineering (David Woods, Erik Hollnagel, Richard Cook):</strong
        >
        Describing how human operators constantly adapt under resource constraints
        to bridge the gap between "work-as-imagined" and "work-as-done."
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of these moral propositions are individually original to
      Ethotechnics. Nor is the mechanism at its center. Others have described
      it closely:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Diane Vaughan, <cite>The Challenger Launch Decision</cite> (1996):</strong>
        at NASA, repeated O-ring erosion was reclassified as acceptable risk
        until the warning stopped registering. She called it the normalization
        of deviance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sidney Dekker, <cite>Drift into Failure</cite> (2011):</strong>
        complex systems move toward failure in small steps, each one locally
        reasonable.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Madeleine Clare Elish, "Moral Crumple Zones" (2019):</strong>
        when an automated system fails, the blame lands on the nearest human
        operator.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Mary L. Gray and Siddharth Suri, <cite>Ghost Work</cite> (2019); Hamid
          Ekbia and Bonnie Nardi, <cite>Heteromation</cite> (2017):</strong
        >
        "automated" systems run on human work that goes unrecorded and is paid
        little or nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Danielle Keats Citron, "Technological Due Process" (2008); Kars
          Alfrink and colleagues, "Contestable AI by Design" (2023):</strong
        >
        automated decisions need notice, a hearing, and a way to contest them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics joins these into one claim: institutions absorb the
      evidence that their automated systems are wrong, and the absorption hides
      the error from the people who could fix the rule. Its vocabulary for that
      mechanism:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong
          >The conversion of human capacity into institutional entitlement:</strong
        >
        Tracing the specific mechanism through which an institution observes that
        people can compensate for its deficiencies, organizes its baseline operations
        on the assumption that they will, and converts an exceptional act of endurance
        into a compulsory operational requirement.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Principle of Non-Expropriation of Resilience:</strong> An individual's
        capacity to adapt to institutional deficiencies does not, by itself, establish
        an institutional entitlement to that adaptation. When a predictable and correctable
        deficiency is routinely compensated for through unrecorded human effort,
        successful outcomes do not establish that the arrangement is adequate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Intrinsic versus compensated institutional performance:</strong>
        Distinguishing performance achieved within formal design models from performance
        parasitic on unrecorded human compensation. This establishes an operational
        boundary: legitimate resilience absorbs irreducible uncertainty with monitored,
        replenished buffers; illegitimate compensation subsidizes predictable, correctable
        design defects.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The epistemic masking feedback loop:</strong> Showing how successful
        adaptation systematically destroys the error signals an institution needs
        to recognize its own defects. Competent human absorption manufactures the
        appearance of system health (the falsified denominator), actively blinding
        the institution to its own operational fragility and blocking structural reform.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Institutional evaluation by burdens eliminated:</strong>
        Evaluating systems not merely by gross output, but by whether a deployment
        or reform reduces the obligation to perform preventable compensatory work
        while preserving substantive freedom and low-cost corrective power.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Its departure is the instrument. The claim becomes obligations a running
      system can fail, records an auditor can read, and public cases scored
      against both:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Obligations with clause numbers:</strong> a grant that expires
        unless renewed on evidence stated in advance (STD-08 §1.1); a widening
        of scope that needs its own authorization (STD-08 §1.3); an objection
        that owes a revision or a reasoned refusal on a clock (STD-07 §4.2); a
        published measure of the time a system costs people (STD-01 §7.1); and
        standing for whoever absorbs its errors (STD-02 §8.4).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Records:</strong> the
        <a href="/standards/std-07-revisable-delegation-record"
          >revisable delegation record</a
        > and the
        <a href="/standards/authority-grant.schema.json">authority grant schema</a
        >, so a reader can check whether each obligation was met.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public cases:</strong> the <a href="/casebook">casebook</a> scores
        {cases.length} failures on the six safeguards and on what the institution
        did afterward: {learning.absorbed} left the process unchanged, {
          learning.partial
        } partly changed it, and {learning.learned} changed the rules.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">
      In short: the underlying political theory establishes why human endurance
      does not legitimate an institutional arrangement; the engineering method
      measures and prevents the mechanisms through which institutions become
      parasitically dependent on that endurance while reporting success.
    </p>
  </section>

